# Views

SwiftUI `View` types are responsible for layout and presentation only. They contain no business logic, no state mutations, and no direct access to services or engines.

## Rules

- A `View` has exactly one primary dependency, which is either:
  - A **view model** (for screens and significant composed UI), or
  - A **model value** (for simple, reusable control views that display or bind a single value).
- Logic that determines what to show, what action to take, or how to transform data belongs in a view model, not in the view.
- Views do not construct their own dependencies. All dependencies are passed in from outside (see [dependency-injection.mdc](.cursor/rules/dependency-injection.mdc)).
- Multi-parameter view initialisers are a signal that logic or wiring is leaking into the view layer.

## Examples

```swift
// ❌ BAD — view takes multiple top-level dependencies (wiring belongs in view model)
struct RunningView: View {
    var viewModel: RunningViewModel
    var peripheralManager: CSCPeripheralManager
    var simulationEngine: SimulationEngine<SystemRandomNumberGenerator>
}

// ❌ BAD — business logic inside the view body
struct RunningView: View {
    var viewModel: RunningViewModel

    var body: some View {
        // deciding what to show based on raw engine state — belongs in view model
        if viewModel.engine.state.vitals.speed.value > 0 {
            SpeedView()
        }
    }
}

// ✅ GOOD — screen view with a single view model dependency
struct RunningView: View {
    @Bindable var viewModel: RunningViewModel

    var body: some View {
        if viewModel.supportsSpeed {
            SpeedView()
        }
    }
}

// ✅ GOOD — reusable control view with a plain model value
struct MetricCardView: View {
    var metric: Metric

    var body: some View {
        Text(metric.formatted)
    }
}
```
